the little valley in South Africa gets  plenty of sunshine as well as rain  perfect conditions for the lush forests  and tea and fruit plantations in the  region it's this fertile landscape that  prompted citrus fruit factory letaba to  set up operations here over 50 years ago  the orange harvest is in full swing the  fruits from the plantation are delivered  to the top a factory they're sorted and  prepared for export or squeezed into  juice the production process is complex  requiring the company to use some 8,000  tons of hard coal each year now the tava  is switching to a greener alternative to  power its operations sawdust it hopes to  save a hundred thousand tons of carbon  emissions in the coming decade CEO under  his friend Vanguard tells us the big  drying oven already runs on sawdust it  comes through in a screw from the screw  it goes down into the oven that year the  lower and from the oven it goes into the  dryer the company emissions does not  exist with with the burning of the  sawdust order or the woodchips as a dust  was there with the coal  unlike coal sawdust is a sustainable  biomass fuel and carbon neutral the tons  of wet fruit peels discarded during  production are sliced and fried with hot  air and a huge drum what comes out is  high-quality animal feed it's perfect  recycling the fruit remains are simply  absorbed back into nature and that's  where the sawdust comes from to just 20  kilometers away from the factory huge  forests on the slopes are home to pine  and eucalyptus to fast growing tree  species it's little surprise then at  forestry is booming in the region the  lumber mills around the valley generate  plenty of wood shavings they form a  mountain of organic waste lumber mill  owner Rohan visagie says Libertas demand  for sawdust is a boon for his company  too I mean if they can use this stuff to  the advantage it's much better than for  me just to leave the stuff lying around  yeah so no it's on a percentage they can  help me to clean my place a bit it  sounds like a real success story but is  it really factory workers and their  families don't seem to have benefited  much from the company's greener  operations Don maaloula key lives in  housing provided by the company it's  totally run down very well not this  house is not good it doesn't have proper  sanitation we're really suffering still  the Potter plans to funnel the money  saved by the sawdust project into a fund  to provide workers with better housing  if the company does manage to link its  climate targets with real benefits for  the workers then it's green project  could indeed be a real success story
 

